{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-99008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-99008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )-(0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-1))-0 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-99008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b45b5bc5368f7920e5bcbfe32e7b4d773349eba09b6a54b0ace547fe5c5e61b"} {"question" "q-99008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-99008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "1-\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\pi +1"] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-99008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104640" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104640" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨1,0,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,0)$ and $(1,0,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,0,2\\pi )-f(1,0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi +1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0)-({1}^{2}\\cdot 0+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(2\\pi +0)-0 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104640" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b45b5bc5368f7920e5bcbfe32e7b4d773349eba09b6a54b0ace547fe5c5e61b"} {"question" "q-104640" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104640" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "\\pi +1"] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104640" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(3{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2x)\\,i+(3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4y)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,e)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({1}^{3}\\cdot {e}^{3}+{1}^{2}-2\\cdot {e}^{2})-({0}^{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}+{0}^{2}-2\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{3}+1-2{e}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={e}^{3}-2{e}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b45b5bc5368f7920e5bcbfe32e7b4d773349eba09b6a54b0ace547fe5c5e61b"} {"question" "q-104725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1+{e}^{2}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}+{e}^{2}+1"] ["math" "{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}"] ["math" "{e}^{3}-2{e}^{2}+3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{3}-2{e}^{2}+3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi ).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}+1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & ={e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}+1}-e \\\\  & =e({e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b45b5bc5368f7920e5bcbfe32e7b4d773349eba09b6a54b0ace547fe5c5e61b"} {"question" "q-104728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "e({e}^{2\\pi }-1)"] ["math" "e({e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}}-1)"] ["math" "{e}^{4\\pi }"] ["math" "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}+1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-104728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e({e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}}-1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-124027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y+1}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={e}^{x}+1$ for $x\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-124027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,2)$ and $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {e}^{3+1}-0\\cdot {e}^{2+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-124027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b45b5bc5368f7920e5bcbfe32e7b4d773349eba09b6a54b0ace547fe5c5e61b"} {"question" "q-124027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-124027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{e}^{4}"] ["math" "4e+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+1"] ["math" "{e}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] ["math" "{e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"} {"question" "q-124027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14078784/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f722674c57a07a7d10b4ae734a511cf614a3b638c6c28266e57ee4757a3d9c7"}] "usage" {"q-99008" []} "basis" 2816 "retractions" [[17592186134487 :question/problem "Let $f(x, y, z) = x\\sin y + y\\cos z$. Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t) = ⟨\\sin t, t, 2t⟩$ for $t ∈ [0, \\frac{π}{2}]$, evaluate $∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186133365 17592186133366 17592186133367 17592186133368 17592186133369] "immutable_fields" [[17592186133364 :answer-field/correct] [17592186133364 :answer-field/id] [17592186133364 :answer-field/key] [17592186133364 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
